Keep the place past a reflowed line on the line's own row

After placing each line, resize_buffer() looks at the position just past
its last cell for a cursor that sits after the text. Computed as one
position after that cell, a line whose last cell was in the last column
named column 0 of the row below -- the next line's row -- so a cursor
there was claimed by the line above it and placed one row too high.

A shell prompt of two lines shows it. Narrowed until the first line
fills a row exactly, the cursor moved onto that row, and the shell's
redraw on SIGWINCH (CR, "> ", erase to end of line) wrote over the
first line's tail and left the real "> " below: the prompt's last line
duplicated. Found from a BeerSSH user's report; 0010 and 0012 inherited
the arithmetic from the loop they replaced.

Keep the place past the end on the last cell's row, with a column that
may equal the row's width.

--- a/src/screen.c
+++ b/src/screen.c
@@ -553,6 +553,29 @@
   return take;
 }
 
+/* Where the cell at `offset` of a reflowed line was, or the place just
+ * past its last cell. Past the end stays on the last cell's row, with a
+ * column that may equal the row's width: computed as one position after
+ * it, a line whose last cell is in the last column named column 0 of the
+ * row BELOW -- the next line's row -- and claimed a cursor that was on
+ * it, one row too high. A narrowed prompt then had its "> " redrawn over
+ * the tail of the line above and left the real one below it. */
+static void reflow_next(const int *cells, int offset, int width, int start_row, int old_cols, int *row, int *col)
+{
+  if(offset < width) {
+    *row = cells[offset] / old_cols;
+    *col = cells[offset] % old_cols;
+  }
+  else if(width) {
+    *row = cells[width - 1] / old_cols;
+    *col = cells[width - 1] % old_cols + 1;
+  }
+  else {
+    *row = start_row;
+    *col = 0;
+  }
+}
